A product is compared only when the match is certain enough: same barcode, or a part-number or name match whose precision was measured against barcodes on the previous run and reached 98% on at least 100 pairs. {% if precision and precision.combined_evaluated %}In the run of {{ day }}, {{ precision.combined_true }} of {{ precision.combined_evaluated }} name and part-number pairs checked against barcodes were the same item.{% else %}Until that bar is reached, only barcode matches are compared ({{ precision.gtin_pairs if precision else 0 }} barcode pairs in the run of {{ day }}).{% endif %} Everything below the bar is listed in [`possible_matches.csv`]({{ blob }}/data/latest/possible_matches.csv) and never compared.

## How does cross-store price matching work?

<p align="center">
  <picture>
    <source media="(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)" srcset="assets/pipeline-still.svg">
    <img src="assets/pipeline.svg" width="760" alt="How it works: 1 Read {{ lg.retailers_ok }} store catalogs ({{ lg.offers_observed | n }} product options). 2 Match by barcode, part number and name. 3 Compare lowest price, gap and stock for {{ comp.products_matched | n }} products. 4 Publish CSV, dashboard and feed, {{ lg.changes_total | n }} changes on {{ day }}.">
  </picture>
</p>

1. **Read.** Once a day, each store's public catalog is read politely: at least 3 seconds between requests to a store, within its robots.txt rules, nothing behind a login.
2. **Match.** The same item at two stores is found by its barcode (GTIN), then by brand and manufacturer part number, then by brand, model words and options. Barcodes are read once per product and refreshed weekly.
3. **Compare.** For every product sold by two or more stores: each store's price and stock, the lowest in-stock price and the gap between highest and lowest.
4. **Publish.** A CSV, a dashboard, a change log and a run feed, with every price and stock change kept in a monthly history file.

